    "text_delta": "ersity policy:\n* Does the hospital have a mandatory rule or guideline requiring gender-balanced or diverse interview panels for senior appointments?\n* If a policy requires panel diversity and an exception was made without formal justification, this is a clear procedural irregularity.\n\n#### C. Request the Scoring Matrix / Benchmark Criteria\nIf the written feedback is vague or generic:\n* Ask for your anonymized score breakdown against the role’s essential and desirable criteria.\n* Depending on your jurisdiction (e.g., Subject Access Requests under GDPR in the UK/EU, public records requests in state-run systems, or union agreements), you may have the right to inspect interview notes and assessment sheets.\n\n#### D. Consult Trusted Advisors Before Escalating\nRaising allegations of gender bias carries professional implications, especially in specialized clinical fields where professional circles are tight:\n* **Union or Professional Body:** If you are a member of a union or professional association (e.g., ACB, IBMS, or local equivalent), consult their workplace representative confidentially. They often know whether similar issues have occurred with this specific employer.\n* **Internal Ombudsperson or Diversity Officer:** If you feel the process was compromised, an informal, confidential conversation with an organizational ombudsperson or EEO advisor can help you understand your options without triggering a formal grievance immediately.\n* **Employment Counsel:** If the written feedback reveals that objective qualifications were ignored in favor of subjective, unmeasured attributes, or if there is documented evidence of inconsistent standards, consult an employment lawyer to evaluate whether a formal grievance or inquiry is warranted.\n",
